  "complexity": 4,
  "title": "Specialist tool hand-over: when [skills] and [tool_disclosure] are both active, the specialist's tool set is the allowlist Claude workers receive; otherwise shadow-logged as today",
  "spec": "Facts (Planner grep on main after PR 22, provenance repo): spawn._shadow_tool_disclosure(task, role, cfg) (spawn.py ~419-440) computes tool_catalog.minimal_set and returns tool_allowlist = \",\".join(choice[\"keep\"]) when [tool_disclosure] mode is active, else the legacy TOOLS[role]; run_worker consumes it at spawn.py ~1233 (disclosure_meta) and launches the worker with that allowlist, then runs the one-shot needs_tool escalation with the legacy allowlist. specialist.compose(task, role, cfg) (specialist.py ~136-215) returns choice[\"specialist\"] with \"tools\" (minimal keep \u222a aliased skill-declared tools; mandatory kept), \"tools_added\", \"tools_unavailable\", \"tools_unknown\"; today those tools are only recorded in the skill_selection decision row (T-0964 v3 kept the hand-over shadow-only because PR 21 was not on the skills branch). skill selection runs once per dispatch before the packet build (T-0939 v3) and its result is available to run_worker.\nDo:\n1. In run_worker (and the Claude execute fallback path), after skill selection and the tool-disclosure choice: when promotion.mode(\"skill_routing\") == \"active\" AND promotion.mode(\"tool_disclosure\") == \"active\" AND the skill routing was not refused (the S5 safety refusal), the allowlist passed to the worker becomes the specialist's tools (choice[\"specialist\"][\"tools\"]: minimal keep \u222a aliased skill tools, mandatory always included, unknown/unavailable never added) instead of the plain minimal keep; record it in the tool_disclosure decision row (selected = the specialist allowlist, reason \"specialist hand-over\", extra = {\"tools_added\": ..., \"skills\": selected ids}) and in packet_meta (\"tool_allowlist_source\": \"specialist\"). In every other mode combination the allowlist is exactly what it is today and the row records \"tool_allowlist_source\": \"minimal\" or \"legacy\".\n2. Escalation stays unchanged: a needs_tool respawn still uses the full legacy allowlist and its budget remainder; the safety refusal of skill routing (fewer than 30 shadow rows or high recovery) forces \"minimal\" as if skills were shadow.\n3. Codex executors are untouched (their tools are the CLI's); record \"tool_allowlist_source\": \"codex\".\n4. docs/skill-intelligence/08-specialists.md: replace the \"shadow-only hand-over\" paragraph with the rule above and the revert path (either mode off restores the previous behaviour); docs/context-economy/06-tool-disclosure.md gets one cross-reference line.\nTests (unittest, `import _harness` first): tests/test_spawn.py::test_specialist_allowlist_used_only_when_both_modes_active (four mode combinations: only skills+tool_disclosure active yields the specialist allowlist; the argv asserted), ::test_specialist_allowlist_keeps_mandatory_and_never_adds_unavailable_tools, ::test_skill_refusal_falls_back_to_minimal_allowlist, ::test_escalation_still_uses_legacy_allowlist_under_hand_over. Never quote double-brace placeholder tokens literally. Do not ask questions; decide and note decisions in the result.",
